September 2026
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Inspired by the exploits of Levent Alpöge (an employee of the artificial intelligence company Anthropic who has recently published several high-profile AI-assisted breakthroughs,
 including a counterexample to the Jacobian conjecture
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\emph on
https://x.com/__alpoge__/status/2079028340955197566
\end_layout

\end_inset

 and the existence
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\emph on
https://x.com/__alpoge__/status/2091639597193368014
\end_layout

\end_inset

 of a complex structure on 
\begin_inset Formula $S^{6}$
\end_inset

),
 ZMD was curious whether publicly available language models could make progress on open problems,
 and prompted Claude Opus 5 to look for candidates in the recent work of Emily Clader.
 Within less than ten minutes,
 the model claimed to have a proof of Conjecture 4.15 in Clader 
\emph on
et al
\emph default
.
 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

Wonderful Compactifications and Rational Curves with Cyclic Action.
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 This writeup represents ZMD's attempt to make sense of the model's work (with extensive tutoring from other Claude instances).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Theorem 4.13 states that for any 
\begin_inset Formula $k\in\mathbb{N}_{0}$
\end_inset

,
 there exists a group isomorphism 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
A^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{L}}_{n}^{r})\cong A^{k}\left(\left(\mathbb{P}^{1}\right)^{n}\right)\oplus\bigoplus_{\ell\ge1}\bigoplus_{\boldsymbol{j}\in\left(\mathbb{N}_{\ge2}\right)^{\ell}}\left(\bigoplus_{\boldsymbol{\mu}\in M_{\boldsymbol{j}}}A^{k-\left|\boldsymbol{\mu}\right|}\left(\left(\mathbb{P}^{1}\right)^{n-\left|\boldsymbol{j}\right|}\right)\right)^{\oplus N_{\boldsymbol{j}}}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
where the absolute value bars denote the sum of the entries of a tuple,
 
\begin_inset Formula $M_{\boldsymbol{j}}:=\{\boldsymbol{\mu}\in\mathbb{Z}^{\ell}:\forall i\in\{1...\ell\}\,1\le\mu_{i}<j_{i}\}$
\end_inset

,
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{\boldsymbol{j}}:=\binom{n}{j_{1},...,j_{\ell},n-\left|\boldsymbol{j}\right|}r^{\left|\boldsymbol{j}\right|}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To be clear,
 I don't really know much about the algebraic geometry here,
 but I'm given to understand that these are free abelian groups:
 if they're isomorphic,
 they have the same rank (and 
\begin_inset Formula $\dim\left(G\oplus H\right)=\dim G+\dim H$
\end_inset

):
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\dim A^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{L}}_{n}^{r})=\dim A^{k}\left(\left(\mathbb{P}^{1}\right)^{n}\right)+\sum_{\ell\ge1}\sum_{\substack{\boldsymbol{j}\in\left(\mathbb{N}_{\ge2}\right)^{\ell}\\
\left|\boldsymbol{j}\right|\le n
}
}N_{\boldsymbol{j}}\sum_{\boldsymbol{\mu}\in M_{\boldsymbol{j}}}\dim A^{k-\left|\boldsymbol{\mu}\right|}\left(\left(\mathbb{P}^{1}\right)^{n-\left|\boldsymbol{j}\right|}\right)
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Then we can form the Poincaré polynomial 
\begin_inset Formula $P_{r,n}(t)$
\end_inset

:
 a generating function in which (for a given 
\begin_inset Formula $r$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

),
 the coëfficient of 
\begin_inset Formula $t^{k}$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula $\dim A^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{L}}_{n}^{r})$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
P_{r,n}(t):=\sum_{k}\dim A^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{L}}_{n}^{r})t^{k}=\sum_{k}\dim A^{k}\left(\left(\mathbb{P}^{1}\right)^{n}\right)t^{k}+\sum_{\ell\ge1}\sum_{\substack{\boldsymbol{j}\in\left(\mathbb{N}_{\ge2}\right)^{\ell}\\
\left|\boldsymbol{j}\right|\le n
}
}N_{\boldsymbol{j}}\sum_{\boldsymbol{\mu}\in M_{\boldsymbol{j}}}\sum_{k}\dim A^{k-\left|\boldsymbol{\mu}\right|}\left(\left(\mathbb{P}^{1}\right)^{n-\left|\boldsymbol{j}\right|}\right)t^{k}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Because 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{P}^{1}\cong S^{2}$
\end_inset

,
 its Poincaré polynomial is 
\begin_inset Formula $1+s^{2}$
\end_inset

,
 but since all the homology is in even degree,
 we use 
\begin_inset Formula $t:=s^{2}$
\end_inset

 and get 
\begin_inset Formula $1+t$
\end_inset

.
 The polynomial of the product of spaces is the product of the polynomials (Künneth),
 so 
\begin_inset Formula $\sum_{k}\dim A^{k}\left(\left(\mathbb{P}^{1}\right)^{p}\right)t^{k}=(1+t)^{p}$
\end_inset

,
 which we can apply to our first summation (with 
\begin_inset Formula $p=n$
\end_inset

).
 The same insight (with 
\begin_inset Formula $p=n-\left|\boldsymbol{j}\right|$
\end_inset

) can be applied to the later summation,
 but we have to reïndex it:
 if we say that 
\begin_inset Formula $k':=k-\left|\boldsymbol{\mu}\right|$
\end_inset

,
 then 
\begin_inset Formula $k=k'+\left|\boldsymbol{\mu}\right|$
\end_inset

.
 Thus 
\begin_inset Formula $t^{k}=t^{k'+\left|\boldsymbol{\mu}\right|}=t^{k'}t^{\left|\boldsymbol{\mu}\right|}$
\end_inset

.
 Then 
\begin_inset Formula $\sum_{k}\dim A^{k-\left|\boldsymbol{\mu}\right|}\left(\left(\mathbb{P}^{1}\right)^{n-\left|\boldsymbol{j}\right|}\right)t^{k}=t^{\left|\boldsymbol{\mu}\right|}\sum_{k'}\dim A^{k'}\left(\left(\mathbb{P}^{1}\right)^{n-\left|\boldsymbol{j}\right|}\right)t^{k'}=t^{\left|\boldsymbol{\mu}\right|}(1+t)^{n-\left|\boldsymbol{j}\right|}$
\end_inset

.
 So we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
(1+t)^{n}+\sum_{\ell\ge1}\sum_{\substack{\boldsymbol{j}\in\left(\mathbb{N}_{\ge2}\right)^{\ell}\\
\left|\boldsymbol{j}\right|\le n
}
}\binom{n}{j_{1},...,j_{\ell},n-\left|\boldsymbol{j}\right|}r^{\left|\boldsymbol{j}\right|}(1+t)^{n-\left|\boldsymbol{j}\right|}\sum_{\boldsymbol{\mu}\in M_{\boldsymbol{j}}}t^{\left|\boldsymbol{\mu}\right|}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Let's interrogate 
\begin_inset Formula $\sum_{\boldsymbol{\mu}\in M_{\boldsymbol{j}}}t^{\left|\boldsymbol{\mu}\right|}$
\end_inset

.
 Every 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{\mu}$
\end_inset

 is an 
\begin_inset Formula $\ell$
\end_inset

-tuple of integers,
 where the 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

th entry is between 1 (inclusive) and 
\begin_inset Formula $j_{i}$
\end_inset

 (exclusive,
 for the 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{j}$
\end_inset

 from an outer summation).
 We have 
\begin_inset Formula $\sum_{\boldsymbol{\mu}\in M_{\boldsymbol{j}}}t^{\left|\boldsymbol{\mu}\right|}=\sum_{\boldsymbol{\mu}\in M_{\boldsymbol{j}}}t^{\sum_{i=1}^{\ell}\mu_{i}}=\sum_{\boldsymbol{\mu}\in M_{\boldsymbol{j}}}\prod_{i=1}^{\ell}t^{\mu_{i}}$
\end_inset

,
 and then interchanging the sum and product by the distributive law,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\sum_{\boldsymbol{\mu}\in M_{\boldsymbol{j}}}\prod_{i=1}^{\ell}t^{\mu_{i}}=\prod_{i=1}^{\ell}\sum_{m=1}^{j_{i}-1}t^{m}$
\end_inset

.
 So we have
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
(1+t)^{n}+\sum_{\ell\ge1}\sum_{\substack{\boldsymbol{j}\in\left(\mathbb{N}_{\ge2}\right)^{\ell}\\
\left|\boldsymbol{j}\right|\le n
}
}\binom{n}{j_{1},...,j_{\ell},n-\left|\boldsymbol{j}\right|}r^{\left|\boldsymbol{j}\right|}(1+t)^{n-\left|\boldsymbol{j}\right|}\prod_{i=1}^{\ell}\sum_{m=1}^{j_{i}-1}t^{m}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For 
\begin_inset Formula $\ell:=0$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\binom{n}{j_{1},...,j_{\ell},n-\left|\boldsymbol{j}\right|}r^{\left|\boldsymbol{j}\right|}(1+t)^{n-\left|\boldsymbol{j}\right|}\prod_{i=1}^{\ell}\sum_{m=1}^{j_{i}-1}t^{m}=\binom{n}{n-0}r^{0}(1+t)^{n-0}=(1+t)^{n}$
\end_inset

,
 so the leading 
\begin_inset Formula $(1+t)^{n}$
\end_inset

 term can be absorbed into the outer summation:
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
P_{r,n}(t)=\sum_{\ell\ge0}\sum_{\substack{\boldsymbol{j}\in\left(\mathbb{N}_{\ge2}\right)^{\ell}\\
\left|\boldsymbol{j}\right|\le n
}
}\binom{n}{j_{1},...,j_{\ell},n-\left|\boldsymbol{j}\right|}r^{\left|\boldsymbol{j}\right|}(1+t)^{n-\left|\boldsymbol{j}\right|}\prod_{i=1}^{\ell}\sum_{m=1}^{j_{i}-1}t^{m}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Also,
 
\begin_inset Formula $r^{\left|\boldsymbol{j}\right|}=r^{\sum_{i=1}^{\ell}j_{i}}=\prod_{i=1}^{\ell}r^{j_{i}}$
\end_inset

,
 which can be merged into the product,
 and we can write out the multinomial coefficient in terms of factorials:
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
P_{r,n}(t)=\sum_{\ell\ge0}\sum_{\substack{\boldsymbol{j}\in\left(\mathbb{N}_{\ge2}\right)^{\ell}\\
\left|\boldsymbol{j}\right|\le n
}
}\frac{n!}{(n-\left|\boldsymbol{j}\right|)!\prod_{i=1}^{\ell}j_{i}!}(1+t)^{n-\left|\boldsymbol{j}\right|}\prod_{i=1}^{\ell}r^{j_{i}}\sum_{m=1}^{j_{i}-1}t^{m}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
At this point,
 we've constructed a generating function 
\begin_inset Formula $P_{r,n}(t)$
\end_inset

 that reproduces individual rows of the table presented in Example 4.14 (where 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 picks out the 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

th entry in the row).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Next,
 we'll construct an exponential generating function 
\begin_inset Formula $F(x,t)$
\end_inset

 that reproduces the entire table (where 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 will pick out the 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

th row).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We begin by forming the exponential generating function by multiplying by 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{x^{n}}{n!}$
\end_inset

 and summing over 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
F(x,t):=\sum_{n\ge0}\sum_{\ell\ge0}\sum_{\substack{\boldsymbol{j}\in\left(\mathbb{N}_{\ge2}\right)^{\ell}\\
\left|\boldsymbol{j}\right|\le n
}
}\frac{\cancel{n!}}{(n-\left|\boldsymbol{j}\right|)!\prod_{i=1}^{\ell}j_{i}!}\frac{x^{n}}{\cancel{n!}}(1+t)^{n-\left|\boldsymbol{j}\right|}\prod_{i=1}^{\ell}r^{j_{i}}\sum_{m=1}^{j_{i}-1}t^{m}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
But note that 
\begin_inset Formula $n=n-\left|\boldsymbol{j}\right|+\sum_{i=1}^{\ell}j_{i}$
\end_inset

,
 and therefore 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{n}=x^{n-\left|\boldsymbol{j}\right|}\prod_{i=1}^{\ell}x^{j_{i}}$
\end_inset

:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
F(x,t)=\sum_{n\ge0}\sum_{\ell\ge0}\sum_{\substack{\boldsymbol{j}\in\left(\mathbb{N}_{\ge2}\right)^{\ell}\\
\left|\boldsymbol{j}\right|\le n
}
}(1+t)^{n-\left|\boldsymbol{j}\right|}\frac{x^{n-\left|\boldsymbol{j}\right|}}{(n-\left|\boldsymbol{j}\right|)!}\prod_{i=1}^{\ell}\frac{x^{j_{i}}}{j_{i}!}r^{j_{i}}\sum_{m=1}^{j_{i}-1}t^{m}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Then we do some more reïndexing:
 let 
\begin_inset Formula $p:=n-\left|\boldsymbol{j}\right|$
\end_inset

.
 Then we can pull out the subexpressions now newly in terms of 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
F(x,t)=\left(\sum_{p\ge0}(1+t)^{p}\frac{x^{p}}{p!}\right)\sum_{\ell\ge0}\sum_{\boldsymbol{j}\in\left(\mathbb{N}_{\ge2}\right)^{\ell}}\prod_{i=1}^{\ell}\frac{x^{j_{i}}}{j_{i}!}r^{j_{i}}\sum_{m=1}^{j_{i}-1}t^{m}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
But by the power series formulation of the natural exponential,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\sum_{p\ge0}(1+t)^{p}\frac{x^{p}}{p!}=e^{(1+t)x}$
\end_inset

 :
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
F(x,t)=e^{(1+t)x}\sum_{\ell\ge0}\sum_{\boldsymbol{j}\in\left(\mathbb{N}_{\ge2}\right)^{\ell}}\prod_{i=1}^{\ell}\frac{x^{j_{i}}}{j_{i}!}r^{j_{i}}\sum_{m=1}^{j_{i}-1}t^{m}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Interchanging the sum and product by the distributive law:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
=e^{(1+t)x}\sum_{\ell\ge0}\prod_{i=1}^{\ell}\sum_{j\ge2}\frac{x^{j}}{j!}r^{j}\sum_{m=1}^{j-1}t^{m}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We have a geometric series:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
e^{(1+t)x}\sum_{\ell\ge0}\left(\sum_{j\ge2}\frac{x^{j}}{j!}r^{j}\sum_{m=1}^{j-1}t^{m}\right)^{\ell}=e^{(1+t)x}\cdot\frac{1}{1-\sum_{j\ge2}\frac{x^{j}}{j!}r^{j}\sum_{m=1}^{j-1}t^{m}}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Which we can further simplify (
\begin_inset Formula $\sum_{m=1}^{j-1}t^{m}$
\end_inset

 is a finite geometric series equal to 
\begin_inset Formula $t\frac{1-t^{j-1}}{1-t}=\frac{t-t^{j}}{1-t}$
\end_inset

):
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{e^{(1+t)x}}{1-\sum_{j\ge2}\frac{(rx)^{j}}{j!}\frac{t-t^{j}}{1-t}}=\frac{e^{(1+t)x}}{1-\sum_{j\ge2}\frac{t(rx)^{j}-(trx)^{j}}{\left(1-t\right)j!}}=\frac{e^{(1+t)x}}{1-\frac{1}{1-t}\left(t\sum_{j\ge2}\frac{(rx)^{j}}{j!}-\sum_{j\ge2}\frac{(trx)^{j}}{j!}\right)}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
=\frac{e^{(1+t)x}}{1-\frac{1}{1-t}\left(t\left(e^{rx}-1-rx\right)-\left(e^{trx}-1-trx\right)\right)}=\frac{e^{(1+t)x}}{1-\frac{1}{1-t}\left(te^{rx}-t\cancel{-trx}-e^{trx}+1\cancel{+trx}\right)}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
=\frac{e^{(1+t)x}}{\underbrace{\frac{1-t}{1-t}}_{``\mathrm{fancy\,one}"}-\frac{1}{1-t}\left(te^{rx}-t-e^{trx}+1\right)}=\frac{e^{(1+t)x}}{\frac{\cancel{1}\cancel{-t}-te^{rx}\cancel{+t}+e^{trx}\cancel{-1}}{1-t}}=\frac{\left(1-t\right)e^{(1+t)x}}{e^{trx}-te^{rx}}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
So we've constructed this exponential generating function 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
F(x,t)=\frac{\left(1-t\right)e^{(1+t)x}}{e^{rtx}-te^{rx}}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Next,
 we're going to use a partial differential equation involving 
\begin_inset Formula $F$
\end_inset

 to construct a recurrence relation for 
\begin_inset Formula $P$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $D:=e^{rtx}-te^{rx}$
\end_inset

 (
\begin_inset Formula $D$
\end_inset

 for the 
\emph on
d
\emph default
enominator of 
\begin_inset Formula $F$
\end_inset

).
 Its partial derivatives are 
\begin_inset Formula $D_{x}=rte^{rtx}-rte^{rx}=rt\left(e^{rtx}-e^{rx}\right)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $D_{t}=rxe^{rtx}-e^{rx}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Now all three of 
\begin_inset Formula $D$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $D_{x}$
\end_inset

,
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $D_{t}$
\end_inset

 are combinations of 
\begin_inset Formula $e^{rtx}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $e^{rx}$
\end_inset

,
 so are linearly dependent (in the sense of a vector space with coëfficients of rational functions of 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

),
 so there should be a relation among them:
 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha D_{x}+\beta D_{t}=\gamma D$
\end_inset

 for some 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

,
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma$
\end_inset

 not all zero.
 Matching the coëfficients of each exponential,
 we have 
\begin_inset Formula $\begin{cases}
\alpha rt+\beta rx=\gamma\\
-\alpha rt-\beta=-t\gamma
\end{cases}$
\end_inset

.
 Negating the second equation and substituting the first into the second,
 we have 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha rt+\beta=\alpha rt^{2}+\beta trx$
\end_inset

,
 which is 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha rt(1-t)=\beta(rtx-1)$
\end_inset

.
 We can choose 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha:=rtx-1$
\end_inset

 to get 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta=rt(1-t)$
\end_inset

.
 Then 
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma=(rtx-1)rt+rt(1-t)rx=\cancel{r^{2}t^{2}x}-rt+r^{2}tx\cancel{-r^{2}t^{2}x}$
\end_inset

,
 so we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
(rtx-1)D_{x}+rt(1-t)D_{t}=rt(rx-1)D
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We know that 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
FD=\left(1-t\right)e^{(1+t)x}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Differentiating (5) with respect to 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

,
 we have 
\begin_inset Formula $F_{x}D+FD_{x}=\left(1+t\right)\underbrace{\left(1-t\right)e^{(1+t)x}}_{FD}=\left(1+t\right)FD$
\end_inset

.
 Then dividing by 
\begin_inset Formula $FD$
\end_inset

 gives 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{F_{x}}{F}+\frac{D_{x}}{D}=1+t$
\end_inset

,
 which reärranges to 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{D_{x}}{D}=1+t-\frac{F_{x}}{F}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
But differentiating (5) with respect to 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

,
 we also have 
\begin_inset Formula $F_{t}D+FD_{t}=-e^{(1+t)x}+\left(1-t\right)xe^{(1+t)x}=-\frac{FD}{1-t}+\cancel{\left(1-t\right)}x\frac{FD}{\cancel{1-t}}=-\frac{FD}{1-t}+xFD=FD\left(-\frac{1}{1-t}+x\right)$
\end_inset

.
 Then dividing by 
\begin_inset Formula $FD$
\end_inset

 gives us 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{F_{t}}{F}+\frac{D_{t}}{D}=-\frac{1}{1-t}+x$
\end_inset

,
 which reärranges to 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{D_{t}}{D}=-\frac{1}{1-t}+x-\frac{F_{t}}{F}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Dividing (4) by 
\begin_inset Formula $D$
\end_inset

 yields 
\begin_inset Formula $(rtx-1)\frac{D_{x}}{D}+rt(1-t)\frac{D_{t}}{D}=rt(rx-1)$
\end_inset

 and substituting yields 
\begin_inset Formula $(rtx-1)\left(1+t-\frac{F_{x}}{F}\right)+rt(1-t)\left(-\frac{1}{1-t}+x-\frac{F_{t}}{F}\right)=rt(rx-1)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We do some more algebra:
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
rtx+rt^{2}x-rtx\frac{F_{x}}{F}-1-t+\frac{F_{x}}{F}+\cancel{-rt}+rt(1-t)x-rt(1-t)\frac{F_{t}}{F}=r^{2}tx\cancel{-rt}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
-rtx\frac{F_{x}}{F}+\frac{F_{x}}{F}-rt(1-t)\frac{F_{t}}{F}=r^{2}tx-rt(1-t)x+1+t-rtx-rt^{2}x=r^{2}tx-rtx+\cancel{rt^{2}x}+1+t-rtx\cancel{-rt^{2}x}=r(r-2)tx+1+t
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Then multiplying by 
\begin_inset Formula $F$
\end_inset

,
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\left(1-rtx\right)F_{x}-rt(1-t)F_{t}=r(r-2)txF+F+tF
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
And reärranging,
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
F_{x}=r(r-2)txF+\left(1+t\right)F+rtxF_{x}+rt(1-t)F_{t}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This partial differential equation for 
\begin_inset Formula $F$
\end_inset

 corresponds to a recurrence relation for 
\begin_inset Formula $P$
\end_inset

.
 For instance,
 
\begin_inset Formula $F_{x}=\sum_{n\ge1}P_{r,n}(t)\frac{x^{n-1}}{\left(n-1\right)!}$
\end_inset

,
 which can be reïndexed to 
\begin_inset Formula $\sum_{n\ge0}P_{r,n+1}(t)\frac{x^{n}}{n!}$
\end_inset

,
 so the coëfficient on 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{x^{n}}{n!}$
\end_inset

 in 
\begin_inset Formula $F_{x}$
\end_inset

 corresponds to 
\begin_inset Formula $P_{r,n+1}$
\end_inset

.
 Similar reasoning leads us to the recurrence
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
P_{r,n+1}(t)=(1+t+rnt)P_{r,n}(t)+rt(1-t)P_{r,n}^{\prime}(t)+r(r-2)ntP_{r,n-1}(t)
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Finally,
 we're ready for 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\emph on
Lemma
\emph default
.
 
\begin_inset Formula $P_{r,n}$
\end_inset

 has 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

 distinct real roots,
 all negative.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\emph on
Proof
\emph default
.
 By induction that 
\begin_inset Formula $P_{r,n}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $P_{r,n+1}$
\end_inset

 each have only negative real roots,
 which 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

interlace
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

:
 if the roots of 
\begin_inset Formula $P_{r,n}$
\end_inset

 are 
\begin_inset Formula $(a_{i})_{i=1}^{n}$
\end_inset

 and the roots of 
\begin_inset Formula $P_{r,n+1}$
\end_inset

 are 
\begin_inset Formula $(b_{i})_{i=1}^{n+1}$
\end_inset

,
 then 
\begin_inset Formula $b_{1}<a_{1}<b_{2}<...<b_{n}<a_{n}<b_{n+1}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
(Base.) 
\begin_inset Formula $P_{r,0}=F(0,t)=\frac{\left(1-t\right)e^{(1+t)0}}{e^{rt0}-te^{r0}}=\frac{1-t}{1-t}=1$
\end_inset

 has no roots,
 which vacuously interlace the one root of 
\begin_inset Formula $P_{r,1}=(1+t+\cancel{0rt})\cancelto{1}{P_{r,0}(t)}+\cancel{rt(1-t)}\cancelto{0}{P_{r,0}^{\prime}(t)}+\cancel{0r(r-2)tP_{r,-1}(t)}=1+t$
\end_inset

,
 which is 
\begin_inset Formula $-1$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
(Induction.) Suppose that 
\begin_inset Formula $P_{r,n}$
\end_inset

 (roots 
\begin_inset Formula $(a_{i})_{i=1}^{n}$
\end_inset

) and 
\begin_inset Formula $P_{r,n+1}$
\end_inset

 (roots 
\begin_inset Formula $(b_{i})_{i=1}^{n+1}$
\end_inset

) have negative interlacing real roots.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We look at 
\begin_inset Formula $P_{r,n+2}$
\end_inset

 evaluated at 
\begin_inset Formula $b_{i}$
\end_inset

:
 
\begin_inset Formula $P_{r,n+2}(b_{i})=\cancel{(1+b_{i}+r(n+1)b_{i})}\cancelto{0}{P_{r,n+1}(b_{i})}+rb_{i}(1-b_{i})P_{r,n+1}^{\prime}(b_{i})+r(r-2)(n+1)b_{i}P_{r,n}(b_{i})$
\end_inset

.
 We know that 
\begin_inset Formula $rb_{i}(1-b_{i})<0$
\end_inset

:
 positive (
\begin_inset Formula $r$
\end_inset

) times negative (
\begin_inset Formula $b_{i}$
\end_inset

) times positive (
\begin_inset Formula $1-b_{i}$
\end_inset

).
 We know that 
\begin_inset Formula $r(r-2)(n+1)b_{i}\le0$
\end_inset

.
 (If 
\begin_inset Formula $r=2$
\end_inset

,
 then 
\begin_inset Formula $r-2$
\end_inset

 is zero.
 Otherwise,
 that's positive times positive times positive times negative.)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $P_{r,n+1}$
\end_inset

 has a leading coëfficient of 
\begin_inset Formula $1$
\end_inset

,
 so it factors as 
\begin_inset Formula $\prod_{i=1}^{n+1}(t-b_{i})$
\end_inset

.
 Differentiating with the product rule produces 
\begin_inset Formula $n+1$
\end_inset

 terms,
 but all but one vanish when we evaluate at 
\begin_inset Formula $t:=b_{i}$
\end_inset

:
 
\begin_inset Formula $P_{r,n+1}^{\prime}(b_{i})=\prod_{j\ne i}(b_{i}-b_{j})$
\end_inset

.
 The factor 
\begin_inset Formula $b_{i}-b_{j}$
\end_inset

 is negative when 
\begin_inset Formula $j>i$
\end_inset

,
 which happens 
\begin_inset Formula $(n+1)-i$
\end_inset

 times,
 so 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathrm{sign}\ P_{r,n+1}^{\prime}(b_{i})=(-1)^{n+1-i}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Then 
\begin_inset Formula $P_{r,n}(t)=\prod_{j=1}^{n}(t-a_{j})$
\end_inset

,
 and we have a similar argument:
 evaluating at 
\begin_inset Formula $b_{i}$
\end_inset

 gives 
\begin_inset Formula $\prod_{j=1}^{n}b_{i}-a_{j}$
\end_inset

.
 The number of negatives in the product is the number of 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{j}$
\end_inset

s greater than 
\begin_inset Formula $b_{i}$
\end_inset

—
which is 
\begin_inset Formula $n-(i-1)=n+1-i$
\end_inset

.
 (The 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{j}$
\end_inset

s go up to 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

,
 but the ones up to 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{i-1}$
\end_inset

 are less than 
\begin_inset Formula $b_{i}$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $b_{i}-a_{j}$
\end_inset

 is positive.) So 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathrm{sign}\:P_{r,n}(b_{i})=(-1)^{n+1-i}$
\end_inset

 as well.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Putting the signs together,
 we see that 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathrm{sign}\ P_{r,n+2}(b_{i})=(-1)^{n+2-i}$
\end_inset

.
 (Either both terms have that sign,
 or the 
\begin_inset Formula $P_{r,n+1}^{\prime}(b_{i})$
\end_inset

 term does and the other is zero.) By the intermediate value theorem,
 each of the intervals 
\begin_inset Formula $(-\infty,b_{1})$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $(b_{1},b_{2})$
\end_inset

 ...
 
\begin_inset Formula $(b_{n},b_{n+1}),(b_{n+1},0)$
\end_inset

 contains exactly one root of 
\begin_inset Formula $P_{r,n+2}$
\end_inset

.
 which we'll call 
\begin_inset Formula $(c_{k})_{k=1}^{n+2}$
\end_inset

,
 which are the 
\begin_inset Formula $n+2$
\end_inset

 roots of 
\begin_inset Formula $P_{r,n+2}$
\end_inset

.
 So we have interlacing 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{1}<b_{1}<c_{2}...<c_{n+1}<b_{n+1}<c_{n+2}$
\end_inset

.
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
But if 
\begin_inset Formula $P_{r,n}$
\end_inset

 has only negative real roots 
\begin_inset Formula $(-d_{i})_{i=1}^{n}$
\end_inset

 and leading coëfficient 1,
 then it's 
\begin_inset Formula $\prod_{i=1}^{n}\left(t+d_{i}\right)$
\end_inset

,
 and has positive coëfficients.
 A well-known result of Newton's inequalities is that if the polynomial 
\begin_inset Formula $\sum_{k=0}^{n}a_{k}t^{k}$
\end_inset

 has only real roots,
 then 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{k}^{2}\ge a_{k-1}a_{k+1}\left(1+\frac{1}{k}\right)\left(1+\frac{1}{n-k}\right)>a_{k-1}a_{k+1}$
\end_inset

,
 and therefore 
\begin_inset Formula $2\log a_{k}\ge\log(a_{k-1}a_{k+1})$
\end_inset

 and therefore 
\begin_inset Formula $\log a_{k}\ge\frac{\log a_{k-1}+\log a_{k+1}}{2}$
\end_inset

,
 which is to say that the Betti numbers of 
\begin_inset Formula $A^{i}(\overline{\mathcal{L}}_{n}^{r})$
\end_inset

 form a log-concave sequence,
 which is 
\emph on
quod erat demonstrandum
\emph default
.
 
\end_layout

